    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Abundant field and petrographic evidence indicates that compaction bands in sandstone can effectively be modeled as closing mode cracks that propagate and interact in accordance with linear elastic theory. In order to verify and refine this hypothesis, we are developing a new 2-D boundary element numerical code in MATLAB􏱥 to simulate anticrack pattern evolution in a two-dimensional linear elastic whole space. We chose MATLAB to take advantage of its well-tested, functionally accessorized and user-friendly computing environment, as well as for its considerable graphic output capabilities.\r\n\r\nThe code is being created to honor our field geology-based understanding of compaction band occurrence and structure, while providing the flexibility to test how different combinations of remote loading, initial geometry, and material and mechanical parameters affect the patterns of band interaction. By calibrating these model pattern results against field examples, we will obtain otherwise unavailable insights into the mechanical nature of this common, but generally overlooked compactive phenomenon, which can reduce permeability and impede production in sandstone reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
